liquid-tower-choices-3 = At the boundary of water and oil
liquid-tower-trick = Most people think a body that sinks in water is bound to end up on the bottom.
liquid-tower-answer-place = { $N } ({ $rho } kg/m³) is denser than every liquid — it went to the bottom.
liquid-tower-answer-place-2 = { $N } ({ $rho } kg/m³) is lighter than every liquid — it floats in the ethanol, { $share } under.
liquid-tower-answer-place-3 = { $N } ({ $rho } kg/m³) went through the lighter liquids and stopped at the { $lw } — { $lw2 } boundary: { $share } of it { $lw3 }, the rest { $lw4 }.
liquid-tower-answer-why =
    The liquids lay in layers by density: a denser liquid goes under a lighter one, as a solid body does. Each body sinks while the liquid round it is lighter than itself — gravity outweighs the buoyant force. As soon as it reaches a liquid denser than itself, the buoyant force exceeds the weight, and the body floats on it. So the body stops at the boundary of two liquids: partly in the lower, partly in the upper — so that the buoyant forces of both together balance the weight. The closer the body's density to the lower liquid's, the deeper it sits in it. That is why "sinks in water" does not yet mean "goes to the bottom": acrylic glass sinks in water, but the glycerol holds it up.

    Glycerol and water do mix: poured with care, the boundary between them holds for a while and then blurs. Ethanol would mix with water too, but the oil is between them. The liquids' volumes are taken to simply add up.
liquid-tower-answer-say = Every body stopped where the liquid beneath it is denser than itself!
